ining: the premise is already covered, and the remainder is out of scope here.\\n\\nThat very test compiles the statement through `new PgDialect().sqlToQuery` at :640 and asserts `document_chat.owner_user_id = $`, `d.id = $`, `d.chat_id = $`, that the owner parameter is bound more than once, and that the chat and document ids are bound. So the owner predicate is pinned at the SQL level despite the `drizzle.mock()` seam.\\n\\nThe other half of cross-tenant enforcement is RLS, which cannot be exercised without a real database — that belongs to the DB-level suite, which the mutation run excludes by design.\"},\n{\"id\":\"PRRT_kwDOO_C_IM6fOT3v\",\"body\":\"This is shipped source behavior, not a test defect — and possibly worth a follow-up on the source.\\n\\n`cropAroundExactAnchor` fixes `contentLimit = CANONICAL_SEARCH_MAX_EXCERPT_CODE_POINTS - 2 = 498` before it knows whether both ellipses will actually be rendered. When the window reaches either end of the source only one ellipsis is emitted, so the excerpt is 499 code points, one under the cap. The cap is an upper bound and is never exceeded, so :176-178 correctly documents what the code does.\\n\\nChanging that is a source change, which this tests-only PR does not make. Flagging it as a follow-up if the single-ellipsis case should fill to 500.\\n\\nSeparately, while in this file I added an adjacent case — `omits the leading ellipsis when the exact window starts at the passage head` — which kills `canonical-search-excerpt.ts:81:18` EqualityOperator `windowStart > 0` → `>= 0`, a live survivor no existing case reached, since every exact-path test produced `windowStart > 0`.\"},\n{\"id\":\"PRRT_kwDOO_C_IM6fOT2w\",\"body\":\"Declining: splitting the fixture would kill nothing.\\n\\nBoth rejection rules are already proven elsewhere. `tool-observation-part.ts:121-124` — the `typeof value.type === 'string'` check, `startsWith` → `endsWith`, the call-id guard and the state equality — has zero surviving mutants in the merged