not affect the score — it closes the reasoning gap you identified rather than a measurable one.\"},\n{\"id\":\"PRRT_kwDOO_C_IM6e13lx\",\"body\":\"The MSI premise does not hold, but the point is fair enough to guard.\\n\\n`canonical-json.ts` has zero surviving mutants, and no Stryker mutator expresses \\\"drop `.update(payload)`\\\" — there is no method-call-deletion mutator, so the payload-blind implementation you describe is not in the generated mutant set.\\n\\n10a21ee7 adds a short assertion that the digest changes for a different payload within one domain, which refutes payload-blindness directly. It kills nothing and is not expected to.\"},\n{\"id\":\"PRRT_kwDOO_C_IM6e13m3\",\"body\":\"Declining the BLOCKING label; the tautology reading does not hold for this mapper.\\n\\n`ProjectResponse` and `Project` have the identical six fields, so `toProjectResponse` is a total identity projection. `toEqual(project)` therefore asserts that every field carries across with the right name and value — that is the mapper's entire contract, and nothing is meant to be stripped or renamed. `projects.dto.ts` has zero surviving mutants in the merged report.\\n\\n10a21ee7 does add a one-line `expect(response).not.toBe(project)`, which refutes the literal \\\"a mapper that returns its input passes\\\" case. It kills nothing.\\n\\nFor context, the two-line source change in this file on the branch is `@IsOptional()` → `@ValidateIf(o => o.archived !== undefined)`, unrelated to the mapper.\"},\n{\"id\":\"PRRT_kwDOO_C_IM6fS4QU\",\"body\":\"Accepted on security merit, and a real gap confirmed — though not one mutation testing can see.\\n\\n`sessions.repository.ts` has zero surviving mutants, so there was nothing to kill. But the IDOR concern stands on its own, and I checked the integration suite: **no** test covers cross-owner session revocation. `auth.integration.test.ts` has `CROSS-TENANT: user B cannot read user A's chat (404)` at :120 and `REVOCATION: after logout, the previously valid